## What does this Google CTR study show? {% #findings %}

The retained query rows recorded 1,667 clicks from 382,941 impressions. Their combined click-through rate was 0.44%. These are observations from a managed portfolio with no country filter or explicit brand exclusion.

The rounded position-one bucket recorded 43 clicks from 721 impressions. Its 5.96% CTR comes from only five query rows. Position four recorded 377 clicks from 4,381 impressions across 24 rows: 8.61% CTR.

The buckets contain different query groups. Their difference does not establish that position four performs better than position one for the same query. Query intent and search-result features were not recorded in the retained aggregate.

Rounded position buckets 1–10 contributed 1,448 of the 1,667 clicks: 86.9%. Buckets 11–20 and 21+ contributed 282,472 impressions: 73.8% of the total. Those are shares of the retained sample rather than shares of all portfolio traffic.

## How was the CTR sample collected? {% #methodology %}

The collection requested query-level Google Search Console data for 9 April–7 July 2026 inclusive. It counted 53 accessible properties with at least one qualifying row. The extraction date recorded in the dataset is 10 July 2026.

The script requested up to 5,000 rows per property in one request. It retained returned rows with at least 50 impressions and an average position of at least 0.5. It made no further requests to retrieve additional pages of rows.

Google sorts query results by clicks in descending order. This cap can favour clicked queries before the impression threshold is applied.

Google also warns that the API returns top rows rather than guaranteeing every data row. See the [Search Analytics query documentation](https://developers.google.com/webmaster-tools/v1/searchanalytics/query).

The collection used the query dimension with the default web search type. It did not filter country or device and did not classify or exclude brand queries. It did not save country shares or query lengths.

The request left aggregation at the API's automatic default. Query-only requests normally aggregate by property, but this extraction did not retain the returned aggregation type. These are not page-level CTR observations.

One row represents a query within a property over the measurement period. The same query can appear in more than one property. The 2,615 rows are therefore not necessarily 2,615 distinct search phrases.

The script counted qualifying Search Console properties as “sites”. The retained output has no property inventory that establishes 53 distinct domains or rules out overlapping domain and URL-prefix properties. The corrected article uses “properties” to describe that count.

## How are position buckets and CTR calculated? {% #calculation %}

Bucket CTR equals total bucket clicks divided by total bucket impressions, multiplied by 100. It is an impression-weighted result. It is neither an average of the row percentages nor an average of the property percentages.

The legacy code rounds average positions below 10.5 to the nearest integer. It assigns positions from 10.5 through 20.5 inclusive to “11–20”.

It assigns positions above 20.5 to “21+”. These exact boundaries are preserved in the download.

The inclusive 20.5 boundary differs from applying ordinary rounding throughout. The retained aggregates cannot show whether any row sat exactly on that boundary. Changing the historical totals without the original rows would invent a correction.

Search Console position is an average over impressions. A query in bucket four did not necessarily hold position four throughout the period.

Google also counts different result formats in ways that make a position bucket an unreliable label for a physical results page. Read Google's [position and impression definitions](https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/7042828).

## What can this dataset not establish? {% #limitations %}

This dataset cannot establish a UK-wide CTR curve or a non-brand benchmark. It cannot measure the effect of AI Overviews, explain zero-click behaviour or predict the gain from moving a particular ranking.

The sample is a convenience collection of accessible properties. Country, device, sector and brand composition were not retained. Properties without qualifying rows do not contribute to the reported count.

The position-one, position-two and position-three buckets contain five, six and 14 rows respectively. Those thin groups should remain visible when quoting their percentages. A large overall impression count does not make each bucket reliable.

The source output contains bucket totals only. It cannot support a per-property spread, a brand split or a robust uncertainty interval. Impressions should not be treated as independent survey respondents.

This correction checks the retained aggregate and extraction script. The original property-query responses and property inventory were not retained in the published artefacts. It is not an independent audit of the underlying Search Console accounts.

The 5,000-row cap and the 50-impression threshold both restrict coverage. Search Console can withhold anonymised queries even when requests are paginated. No extraction coverage ratio or overlap audit was saved with this edition.

## How should you use these CTR figures? {% #use-the-data %}

Use the figures to inspect how a declared sample was aggregated. Use your own comparable country, device, query and page data for forecasts. Keep clicks and impressions beside every percentage.

Compare matching segments over defined periods. Record title changes, ranking changes and other changes that could affect the comparison. An observed improvement alone does not isolate its cause.

For a business case, test a range of assumptions against contribution profit and qualified outcomes. The [SEO ROI calculator](/tools/seo-roi-calculator/) supports scenario planning. This study does not supply a universal expected CTR for that calculation.

## What would a stronger repeat study require? {% #next-edition %}

A stronger study requires a fixed authorised property panel, an explicit UK country filter and a declared brand-classification rule. Device splits, coverage checks and minimum reporting thresholds should be specified before analysis.

Choose one non-overlapping property definition per site. Record inclusion, attrition and failed requests. Retain an anonymised audit trail privately so the published counts can be checked without exposing domains or search queries.

Retrieve additional API pages where available and compare returned totals with matching property totals. Document anonymised-query limits and truncation. Apply the impression threshold consistently and inspect how different thresholds affect the results.

Publish the new edition separately with its own dates and method. A filtered UK panel must not be spliced into this unfiltered historical series. The current repair changes interpretation and access to the data; it is not a new collection.

## What was corrected in September 2026? {% #corrections %}

The 12 September 2026 correction removes unsupported UK-only and non-brand descriptions. It replaces causal ranking claims with sample observations. The original measurement dates and all 12 bucket totals remain unchanged.

The earlier article attributed differences between buckets to query intent and AI features without collecting those variables. It also suggested that moving from one bucket to another would multiply CTR. Those explanations and forecasts have been withdrawn.

The corrected citation names 53 qualifying properties and 2,615 query rows. The downloads include the extraction limits and precise bucket boundaries. Send a correction with the relevant table row and evidence through the [contact page](/contact/).
